[1] In 1943, Richard James, a mechanical engineer at a Philadelphia shipyard, was designing springs to keep delicate instruments steady on ships at {{1|sea. When}} one of his springs fell from a shelf. [2] Instead of landing in a heap, the spring stepped end over end across the floor, {{2|it's coils}} flipping one over another. [3] James spent the next two years testing different steels and coil sizes {{3|until he finds}} a spring that would walk reliably.

[4] His wife, Betty, searched the dictionary for a name and settled on "Slinky," a word meaning sleek and graceful. [5] In November 1945, the couple persuaded Gimbels, a Philadelphia department store, to let them demonstrate the {{4|toy; they}} worried, though, that no one would buy it. [6] They need not have worried: within ninety minutes, all four hundred of the Slinkys they had brought {{5|was sold}}. {{7|}} [7] {{6|Nevertheless,}} the toy, priced at one dollar, was being sold in stores across the country.

[8] Made from a single length of steel wire, {{8|children discovered that the toy could}} do far more than walk down stairs. [9] It could be stretched across a room, bounced like a yo-yo, or used by teachers to show how a wave travels. [10] {{9|Many other popular toys have also been invented by accident.}}

[1] In 1816, the Scottish scientist David Brewster was studying how light behaves when it reflects off glass. [2] While experimenting with two mirrors placed at an angle, he noticed that small objects between {{1|them was repeated}} in a symmetrical pattern. [3] Brewster built a tube around the mirrors, {{2|adding colored glass at one end, and}} called the device a kaleidoscope, from Greek words meaning "beautiful form" and "to look at."

[4] {{3|Brewster patented the device in 1817, however, his patent}} had a flaw. [5] A mistake in the paperwork left the design poorly protected, and copies appeared in shops {{4|almost immediately}}. [6] Within months, an enormous number of {{5|kaleidoscopes was sold}} in London and Paris. [7] Brewster{{6|, however}} earned little from them. {{7|}}

[8] Although he was disappointed, Brewster continued his research on light, which earned him many scientific honors. [9] Today he is remembered less for that research than for a toy {{8|that he hoped would also be useful to artists and designers}}.
